NSS-HQC implementations
=======================

This directory contains the NSS-HQC KEM implementations for the API_PKC
submission package.

The implementation package contains both a reference implementation and a
behavior-aligned optimized implementation for four parameter sets:

- HQC-128
- HQC-256
- HQC-384
- HQC-512


Directory layout
----------------

The implementation directory is organized as follows:

    Implementations/
      README
      Reference_Implementation/
        HQC-128/
        HQC-256/
        HQC-384/
        HQC-512/
      Optimized_Implementation/
        HQC-128/
        HQC-256/
        HQC-384/
        HQC-512/

The corresponding known-answer test files are provided in the package-level
Test_Vectors directory:

    Test_Vectors/
      KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt
      KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt
      KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt
      KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt


Implementation trees
--------------------

Reference_Implementation contains the portable reference implementation.

Optimized_Implementation is behavior-aligned with Reference_Implementation for
all four NSS-HQC instances. It preserves the same external API, parameter
sizes, ciphertext format, and KAT behavior.

Each implementation tree contains four parameter-set directories:

    HQC-128/
    HQC-256/
    HQC-384/
    HQC-512/

Each parameter-set directory contains the API_PKC KEM interface files:

    KAT_KEM.c
    KEM_AlgorithmInstance.c
    KEM_AlgorithmInstance.h
    auxfunc.c
    auxfunc.h
    drng.c
    drng.h
    Makefile

and the NSS-HQC algorithm source files used by that instance.


API_PKC interface
-----------------

Each instance uses the API_PKC KEM_AlgorithmInstance interface:

    kem_get_pk_len_bytes
    kem_get_sk_len_bytes
    kem_get_ss_len_bytes
    kem_get_ct_len_bytes
    kem_keygen
    kem_enc
    kem_dec

The API_PKC template files KAT_KEM.c, auxfunc.c/.h, and drng.c/.h are kept in
each instance directory.

The submitted KEM sizes are:

    HQC-128: pk = 3713,  sk = 3777,  ss = 32,  ct = 5185
    HQC-256: pk = 6844,  sk = 6908,  ss = 32,  ct = 9084
    HQC-384: pk = 15371, sk = 15467, ss = 48,  ct = 18571
    HQC-512: pk = 27302, sk = 27430, ss = 64,  ct = 31462

Here ct denotes the full KEM ciphertext length.


Build environment
-----------------

The implementations were built and tested on Windows with MSYS2 UCRT64.

Open an MSYS2 UCRT64 shell and check the environment with:

    echo $MSYSTEM
    which gcc
    gcc --version
    which make
    make --version

The expected MSYS2 environment is UCRT64, with gcc available under /ucrt64/bin.


Experiment 1: single-instance build and self-test
-------------------------------------------------

This experiment checks whether one selected instance can be compiled and run
through its self-test.

For example, to test the reference HQC-128 instance:

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-128
    make clean
    make selftest

The same procedure can be repeated for every other instance:

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-256
    make clean
    make selftest

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-384
    make clean
    make selftest

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-512
    make clean
    make selftest

and for the optimized implementation tree:

    cd Implementations/Optimized_Implementation/HQC-128
    make clean
    make selftest

    cd Implementations/Optimized_Implementation/HQC-256
    make clean
    make selftest

    cd Implementations/Optimized_Implementation/HQC-384
    make clean
    make selftest

    cd Implementations/Optimized_Implementation/HQC-512
    make clean
    make selftest

A successful self-test means that key generation, encapsulation, and
decapsulation complete successfully and that the shared secrets match on valid
ciphertexts.


Experiment 2: KAT generation for one instance
---------------------------------------------

This experiment generates the known-answer test file for one selected instance.

For example, to generate the KAT for the reference HQC-128 instance:

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-128
    make clean
    make kat

The generated KAT file is written to the instance output directory:

    output/KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt

The same procedure is used for HQC-256, HQC-384, and HQC-512 by entering the
corresponding parameter-set directory and running:

    make clean
    make kat


Experiment 3: KAT generation for all eight instances
----------------------------------------------------

This experiment generates KAT files for all reference and optimized instances.

In MSYS2 UCRT64, from the package root, run:

    cd Implementations/Reference_Implementation/HQC-128
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-256
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-384
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-512
    make clean
    make kat

Then run the same commands for the optimized tree:

    cd ../../Optimized_Implementation/HQC-128
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-256
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-384
    make clean
    make kat

    cd ../HQC-512
    make clean
    make kat

After this experiment, each instance has a generated KAT file under its own
output directory.


Experiment 4: Reference/Optimized KAT comparison
------------------------------------------------

This experiment checks that the reference and optimized implementations produce
byte-identical KAT files.

On Windows PowerShell, from the package root, run:

    fc.exe /b Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-128\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt Implementations\Optimized_Implementation\HQC-128\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt

    fc.exe /b Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-256\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt Implementations\Optimized_Implementation\HQC-256\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt

    fc.exe /b Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-384\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt Implementations\Optimized_Implementation\HQC-384\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt

    fc.exe /b Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-512\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt Implementations\Optimized_Implementation\HQC-512\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt

The expected result for each comparison is:

    FC: no differences encountered

or the corresponding localized Windows message indicating that no differences
were found.


Experiment 5: submitted Test_Vectors verification
-------------------------------------------------

The final submitted KAT files are collected under the package-level
Test_Vectors directory:

    Test_Vectors/KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt
    Test_Vectors/KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt
    Test_Vectors/KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt
    Test_Vectors/KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt

The submitted Test_Vectors files are checked against the reference output files
with:

    fc.exe /b Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-128\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt

    fc.exe /b Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-256\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt

    fc.exe /b Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-384\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt

    fc.exe /b Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt Implementations\Reference_Implementation\HQC-512\output\KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt

Each comparison should report no differences.


Experiment 6: KAT format and length verification
------------------------------------------------

Each KAT file follows the API_PKC KAT format. Each record contains fields such
as:

    Count
    Seed_Len
    Seed
    PK_Len
    PK
    SK_Len
    SK
    SS_Len
    SS
    CT_Len
    CT

The expected length fields are:

    HQC-128: PK_Len = 3713,  SK_Len = 3777,  SS_Len = 32, CT_Len = 5185
    HQC-256: PK_Len = 6844,  SK_Len = 6908,  SS_Len = 32, CT_Len = 9084
    HQC-384: PK_Len = 15371, SK_Len = 15467, SS_Len = 48, CT_Len = 18571
    HQC-512: PK_Len = 27302, SK_Len = 27430, SS_Len = 64, CT_Len = 31462

On Windows PowerShell, these fields can be checked by inspecting the beginning
of a KAT file, for example:

    Get-Content Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt -TotalCount 30

The expected beginning of a KAT file contains:

    Count = 0
    Seed_Len = 64
    Seed = ...
    PK_Len = ...
    PK = ...
    SK_Len = ...
    SK = ...
    SS_Len = ...
    SS = ...
    CT_Len = ...
    CT = ...


Experiment 7: KAT SHA256 verification
-------------------------------------

The SHA256 hashes of the submitted KAT files are:

    HQC-128:
    66A0FE8816A982506B0D799C43FCDD18D0434FC54B12FC1E2CC50C0603AF8BFC

    HQC-256:
    8CDFACEA8444ED8E1380B9CE3CDB4F1EE270ACFD35BCD39BB50AC3C4E00C9454

    HQC-384:
    9CE17CCF09E019AB564A4FE0F5799B52054DC8F865DEC790C69AA272188DC8E5

    HQC-512:
    E3D01CB3F70F06C548AB50CDEE8028CD53974789572DE9604ACDB7E5A4A9D3D1

They can be verified on Windows PowerShell with:

    Get-FileHash Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-128.txt -Algorithm SHA256
    Get-FileHash Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-256.txt -Algorithm SHA256
    Get-FileHash Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-384.txt -Algorithm SHA256
    Get-FileHash Test_Vectors\KAT_KEM_HQC-512.txt -Algorithm SHA256


Experiment 8: external API smoke test
-------------------------------------

This experiment checks the external KEM_AlgorithmInstance API.

For each instance, a small external test program can include
KEM_AlgorithmInstance.h, allocate buffers according to the returned lengths,
and call:

    kem_keygen
    kem_enc
    kem_dec

The test checks:

1. kem_keygen returns success;
2. kem_enc returns success;
3. kem_dec returns success on a valid ciphertext;
4. the shared secret from encapsulation matches the shared secret from
   decapsulation;
5. decapsulation on a tampered ciphertext does not crash.

This verifies that the submitted API can be called externally through the
API_PKC KEM_AlgorithmInstance interface.


Summary of validation
---------------------

The submitted NSS-HQC implementation was validated as follows:

1. All eight instances compile and run self-tests.
2. All eight instances generate API_PKC-format KAT files.
3. Reference and optimized KAT files are byte-identical for all four parameter
   sets.
4. The package-level Test_Vectors files are byte-identical to the reference
   generated KAT files.
5. The KAT length fields match the submitted NSS-HQC parameter sizes.
6. The external KEM_AlgorithmInstance API smoke test passes for all eight
   instances.